"adr-2608136000"]
  :adr/body "## Context\n\nOwner instruction (2026-09-10): point the hermes profile bots' API at\nmurakumo.cloud and get them running stably.\n\nMeasured before changing anything. 235 profiles under `~/.hermes`, 240 cron\njobs (236 enabled), 770 scheduled runs/day. 227 of 231 profile configs named\n`z-ai/glm-5.3-flash` on OpenRouter. 98 jobs carried `failure_streak > 0`, and\n77 of them carried the same `last_error`:\n\n    RuntimeError: HTTP 402: This request requires more credits, or fewer\n    max_tokens.\n\n`GET https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/credits` answered `total_credits 2386`,\n`total_usage 2375.998` -- **$10.00 left**. The fleet was not misconfigured.\nIt had run out of money. That is the same instruction read from the other\nside, and it is why this was urgent rather than cosmetic.\n\n## Decision\n\n`scripts/hermes-murakumo-api.cljs` owns the fleet's inference contract as one\nmap and renders `~/.hermes/config.yaml`, all 231 profile configs and all 240\ncron job records from it. `--apply` writes, `--verify` measures, `--self-test`\ncounts (37 assertions), and every owned block is re-rendered whole while every\nunowned top-level key is carried through verbatim.\n\n    base_url            https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1\n    model               murakumo-main        (the ALIAS, per ADR-2607173100)\n    max_tokens          2048\n    context_length      65536\n    cron.max_parallel_jobs 6                 (default was UNBOUNDED)\n    fallback            openrouter-free / z-ai/glm-5.3-flash\n\nWhy murakumo-main and not any other name: the endpoint serves fleet-hosted\nmodels anonymously and 402s everything else. Measured 2026-09-10, each 402\nnaming `hosted_alternative: murakumo-main`:\n\n    z-ai/glm-5.3-flash   402 external_model_requires_payment\n    anthropic/claude-*   402 external_model_requires_payment\n    awai-network/basho   402 self_model_requires_identity\n\nThe 402 body advertises rails (`POST /v1/murakumo/token`, passkey, CACAO),\nbut that path answers **404**, so the paid lane is not reachable today.\n\n24 profiles were ALREADY pointed at `api.murakumo.cloud` with\n`model.default: z-ai/glm-5.3-flash` -- pointed at it with a name it refuses\nthem. A cutover half-done long enough to look finished.\n\nWhy 2048 max_tokens: `/ready` reports `request-capacity {configured 2}` for\nthe whole fleet and measured decode is 13-17 tok/s. 16384 tokens is up to 18\nminutes of one of two slots; 2048 is about 140s.\n\nWhy 6 parallel jobs: measured from 19,033 execution rows -- median run 133s,\nmean 398s. 770 runs/day x 398s = 3.55 job-seconds per second, so the pool was\nalready averaging ~3.5 concurrent jobs with nothing capping it. Set in the\ngateway plists as `HERMES_CRON_MAX_PARALLEL` (env beats config in\n`_resolve_max_parallel_workers`, and survives a config rewrite); the pool is\nmodule-global per gateway PROCESS, so the main multiplexed gateway gets 6 and\neach of the 4 dedicated ones gets 2.\n\nTwo cron jobs (`hyakka/hyakka-model-refresh`, `hyakka-corpus/…`, the latter\nhourly at :40) run `refresh_free_model.py`, which installs an OpenRouter free\nmodel as PRIMARY by rewriting `~/.hermes/config.yaml`. They are **disabled**,\nwith the reason written into the job record. Not deleted: the resolver is\nstill the right answer if the fleet endpoint is retired.\n\n## The blocker, measured rather than inferred\n\nRunning a real job -- not reading the config back -- found two things:\n\n1. `context_length: 16384`, which is b70's live `slot-context` and the\n   obviously correct value, is refused by **Hermes**, not by murakumo:\n\n       ValueError: Model murakumo-main has a context window of 16,384\n       tokens, which is below the minimum 64,000 required by Hermes Agent.\n\n   `MINIMUM_CONTEXT_LENGTH = 64_000` in `agent/model_metadata.py`, enforced\n   in `agent_init.py::_enforce_minimum_context`, waived only for lmstudio.\n   Hence 65536 -- the smallest value Hermes accepts, not a claim about what\n   the fleet can hold.\n\n2. With that fixed the request reached the endpoint and returned\n   `502 murakumo fleet unreachable: Error: connection_refused` in 0.2s.\n   Replaying Hermes's exact body with curl reproduced it, so it is the\n   request and not the client. Bisecting a synthetic prompt found a sharp,\n   repeatable boundary:\n\n       3,018 prompt tokens (15,115 body bytes)    HTTP 200\n       ~3,200 prompt tokens (15,615 body bytes)   HTTP 502\n\n   The 502 carries no `x-murakumo-route-head` header at all: the router\n   picked no head. Far above the band the gateway rejects cleanly with\n   `400 Input reserve exceeds this model's serving context window`.\n\n   Hermes's MINIMUM request -- system prompt plus 19 tool schemas, before a\n   single conversation turn -- measured **4,459 tokens**, which is above the\n   ceiling. `/ready` reports `pool-available {gad 0, b70 2, xavier 1}` and\n   `degraded [\"fleet-incomplete\" \"primary-unavailable\"]`; `capacity-members`\n   gives gad 262144, b70 16384, xavier 8192. **gad is the only head that\n   clears Hermes's floor and it has zero free slots.**\n\nSo while this holds, api.murakumo.cloud cannot serve a Hermes agent at all,\nand no setting in the config changes that. 17 of the 234 enabled jobs are\n`no_agent` scripts that never call an LLM; the other 217 all send that prefix.\n\n## Current state\n\nThe fleet runs. `cron-health-report` was run for real and returned `ok`, but\nthe honest reading of that green is in the transcript line above it:\n\n    Model fallback: murakumo-main via custom unavailable (provider failure);\n    using z-ai/glm-5.3-flash via openrouter-free.\n\nIt succeeded by falling back, not by murakumo serving. That fallback only\nexists because a profile's `config.yaml` REPLACES the root's rather than\nlayering over it -- the first cutover put the chain in the root only, and\n`hermes --profile cron-health fallback list` then said \"No fallback providers\nconfigured\" while a 502 (`FailoverReason.server_error`: retry, then arm\nfallback) died terminally with nothing to arm.\n\n**murakumo.cloud is the configured API. It is not yet the serving one.**\nClosing that gap is upstream of this repo: a head that clears 64K back in the\npool (gad), or a raised per-request ceiling on the gateway. Until then the\n217 agent jobs ride the OpenRouter fallback, which had $10.00 left.\n\n## Verification\n\n| what | result |\n|---|---|\n| `--self-test` | 37 assertions, ok. Each of 6 deliberate breaks went red for its own named assertion; restoring went green |\n| rehearsal on a full copy of `~/.hermes` | 231 configs + 240 jobs rewritten; second apply changed 0 -- idempotent |\n| `--verify` after apply | exit 0, no findings; root `model.default` = murakumo-main |\n| endpoint probe | `/ready` 200, chat/completions 200, tool_call returned |\n| heads clearing Hermes's 64K floor | **NONE** |\n| `hermes --profile cron-health fallback list` | primary murakumo-main, 1 fallback entry |\n| real job run | `ok`, **via fallback** |\n| `launchctl print` on all 5 gateways | `HERMES_CRON_MAX_PARALLEL` present in the LOADED definition |\n\nThe launchctl step is recorded because the first attempt did not take: 4 of 5\n`bootstrap` calls returned `5: Input/output error` while the surrounding\noutput still read as success, and only `launchctl print` showed the old\ndefinition still loaded. Booting out, waiting for the domain to actually\nclear, then bootstrapping fixed it -- the same failure ADR-2609..., the gftd\ncutover, recorded.\n\n## Not done, and named\n\n- **31 cron jobs name a `workdir` under `~/.gftd`**, the retired home. The\n  `.itonami` equivalents do not exist either; these are removed git worktrees.\n  Left alone: 24 of them last ran OK because a job with a missing workdir\n  falls back to its profile directory. Out of scope for an API change.\n- `com.gftd.hermes-budget-guard.plist` still writes its log to\n  `~/.gftd/hermes-budget-guard/guard.log`, which **recreates the retired\n  directory** on every 5-minute tick.\n- One stranded execution row (`manager/bc3ae98126e9`, dead pid, 23h) and one\n  dangling `:script` (`fleet-kaizen/fleet-kaizen-audit.py`), both already\n  reported by `verify-hermes-profile-fitness`.\n"}]
